WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:02.008
Born and raised in Texas, Jeb

00:00:02.009 --> 00:00:04.499
Bush moved to Florida in 1980.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:06.189
The son a  nd brother of presidents.

00:00:06.190 --> 00:00:07.619
He himself was governor of the

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:09.499
Sunshine State from 1999

00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:11.489
to 2007, where

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:12.929
he quickly became known as a

00:00:12.930 --> 00:00:14.939
champion of school choice and fiscal

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:16.078
responsibility.

00:00:16.079 --> 00:00:18.509
In 2016, he had an unsuccessful

00:00:18.510 --> 00:00:19.979
run for president, and he now

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:21.429
resides in Miami-Dade County,

00:00:21.430 --> 00:00:23.309
happily retired from

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:25.409
political life, a self-proclaimed

00:00:25.410 --> 00:00:27.149
old school conservative with

00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:28.709
libertarian blood running through

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:29.519
his veins.

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:31.319
I talked to Bush in October for a

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:33.239
special issue of reason

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:35.549
devoted to all things Florida.

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:37.169
He told me the reasons he believes

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:38.759
more people are moving to Florida

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:40.049
than any other state.

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:41.969
What he thinks of current Governor

00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:44.219
Ron DeSantis and leading Republican

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:46.139
presidential candidate Donald Trump.

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:48.329
His problems and they are many

00:00:48.330 --> 00:00:49.769
with President Joe Biden.

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:51.389
And what's so special about

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:53.429
immigration and America?

00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:00.479
Governor Jeb Bush, thanks for

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:01.480
talking to Reason.

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:03.100
Honored to be with you, Nick.

00:01:03.990 --> 00:01:06.659
So let's start with

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:09.029
the question of, you know, Florida

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:10.889
has become the fastest

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:12.719
growing state in the country.

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:14.639
What explains Florida's growth

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:16.889
as a destination for people

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:18.599
wanting to move there and live there

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:19.600
and work there?

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:23.999
I think Florida works pretty good.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:25.529
It's like whatever problems we have

00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:27.419
pale by comparison to two other

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:29.281
states. And I mean like you, no

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:32.009
disrespect to the Northeast, but if

00:01:32.010 --> 00:01:33.959
you have business up there like

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:36.089
I do and you go and you drive

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:37.859
like you're going to get a broken

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:39.779
back, basically trying to go

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:41.069
from here to there because the

00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:42.070
infrastructure is

00:01:43.110 --> 00:01:44.999
decayed, the challenges

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:46.859
are immense. And you come here and

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:48.239
the roads, the roads are working.

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:49.919
They've got a lot of people on them,

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:52.679
but fewer potholes.

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:53.789
Things work better.

00:01:53.790 --> 00:01:55.649
And so it's for people

00:01:55.650 --> 00:01:56.459
that come to visit.

00:01:56.460 --> 00:01:58.289
They can kind of get the allure

00:01:58.290 --> 00:01:59.759
of Florida. And then typically what

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:01.229
happens is they visit a few times

00:02:01.230 --> 00:02:02.399
and then they say, all right, I'm

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:03.659
making the.

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:04.679
When making the jump.

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:06.329
When did you move there?

00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:07.859
What year?

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:08.819
1980.

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:10.019
1980.

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:12.029
And Florida has at least since

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:13.889
the I mean, in the postwar era, but

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:15.839
definitely in the 60s, 70s,

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:18.029
80s, 90s and continuing

00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:20.369
just saw really kind of explosive

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:21.179
growth.

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:23.039
Texas has also where you

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:24.449
grew up. You went to school at

00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:26.639
University of Texas Austin.

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:27.059
Yeah.

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:28.979
I hope that they lose in

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:30.989
whatever, you know football games

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:32.039
that they have coming up this

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:34.259
season. I'm rooting for Ohio State.

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:36.149
Be that as it may.

00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:39.119
Both Texas and Florida,

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:41.729
two states that have become reliably

00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:43.889
red, are now kind of kicking

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:46.679
the butt of New York and California.

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:48.449
These four states are the four most

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:50.759
populous states in the country.

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:53.849
Is there something going on

00:02:53.850 --> 00:02:55.799
where the general kind

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:57.659
of political

00:02:57.660 --> 00:02:59.579
system in Florida

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:01.469
and Texas is being

00:03:01.470 --> 00:03:03.719
revealed as better than

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:05.159
the ones that are in place in

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:07.079
California, in New York.

00:03:07.080 --> 00:03:09.509
In Miami Dade County, where I live,

00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:11.039
has 2.5 million people.

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:12.899
It's a big urban, teeming

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:14.819
place full of

00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:15.929
diversity.

00:03:15.930 --> 00:03:17.759
It has a 1.4%

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:19.049
unemployment rate.

00:03:19.050 --> 00:03:21.089
I mean, effectively, it used to be,

00:03:21.090 --> 00:03:22.529
Nick, we're old enough to remember

00:03:22.530 --> 00:03:24.479
full employment was like at 4%.

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:26.909
Oh, yeah. So, I mean, people are

00:03:26.910 --> 00:03:28.229
half the people have two jobs, I

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:29.609
guess. I don't know. It's like it's

00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:31.470
it's full of opportunities.

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:35.129
The tax code matters, obviously,

00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:37.019
for people with

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:38.369
more wealth and for sure.

00:03:38.370 --> 00:03:40.259
We don't have income tax.

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:42.419
We have smaller governments.

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:44.039
The impulse isn't to later try to

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:45.659
tax your way to prosperity or

00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:47.309
regulate your way to prosperity.

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:48.959
It's not a perfect place for sure.

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:50.309
I get nervous about bragging too

00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:52.169
much because the minute you

00:03:52.170 --> 00:03:53.999
stop challenging

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:55.649
basic assumptions about the role of

00:03:55.650 --> 00:03:57.569
government is you're after

00:03:57.570 --> 00:03:59.489
few sets in and government

00:03:59.490 --> 00:04:01.349
kind of just starts filling the

00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:02.219
seams.

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:04.169
And so we've

00:04:04.170 --> 00:04:06.089
had a great run because we've had a

00:04:06.090 --> 00:04:07.800
period of limited government

00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:10.799
and we don't try to

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:12.569
micromanage people's lives.

00:04:12.570 --> 00:04:14.249
And as long as we do that and we

00:04:14.250 --> 00:04:16.469
stay focused on the important

00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:18.509
long term things, and I would say

00:04:18.510 --> 00:04:19.559
makes it making sure our

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:21.359
infrastructure stays up with the

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:23.369
growth, making sure our natural

00:04:23.370 --> 00:04:26.069
systems are protected, which is an

00:04:26.070 --> 00:04:28.049
important conservative principle

00:04:28.050 --> 00:04:29.789
in my mind is, you know, we could

00:04:29.790 --> 00:04:32.639
rape and pillage the the

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:34.709
the swamplands around South Florida

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:35.819
and you would have a hell of a

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:37.259
problem going forward.

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:39.149
So investing in these long term

00:04:39.150 --> 00:04:40.799
things, which they don't do in

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:42.119
California and New York because

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:44.009
they've made 20, 30 years

00:04:44.010 --> 00:04:45.899
ago, they make obligations

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:47.789
to the unions and obligations

00:04:47.790 --> 00:04:49.559
in the here and now, that that

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:51.269
creates a government that is far

00:04:51.270 --> 00:04:53.099
bigger than it is in Texas or

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:54.059
in Florida.

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:56.369
Talk a bit more about conservation

00:04:56.370 --> 00:04:57.719
efforts or the environment in

00:04:57.720 --> 00:04:59.699
Florida is a big deal

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:01.139
because, you know, it's a state that

00:05:01.140 --> 00:05:02.819
is surrounded by water.

00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:04.259
You know, three sides, right.

